# Surface abstraction

The `Surface` interface is the seam between "how we perceive and act on a
concrete UI" and "the recorded flow." This is what lets an artifact
generalize from a web app to a legacy web app or a desktop app without
changing its schema.

```ts
interface Surface {
  observe(): Promise<Observation>   // compressed, interactive-only node list
  act(call: ActionCall): Promise<ActionResult>
  screenshot(): Promise<Buffer>
}
```

## Perception channel: accessibility tree, not raw DOM

The primary observation format is a compressed accessibility-tree snapshot,
not raw HTML/DOM. Example:

```
[ref=12] textbox "Member ID"        (frame: content)
[ref=13] button  "Search"           (frame: content)
[ref=20] table   "Accounts"  rows=3 (frame: content)
   [ref=21] cell "Savings"  | [ref=22] cell "$4,210.55"
```

- Only interactive or named nodes are included (cap ~50 nodes) — this both
  keeps the model's context small and forces the artifact's locators toward
  the `role + name` strategy that is portable across surfaces.
- `ref` numbers are per-observation and generated by a **stable traversal
  order** of the accessibility tree — not an incrementing counter — so that
  identical page states hash identically for the LLM cassette cache
  (see cost-control.mdc).
- Do not attach screenshots to observations by default; only attach one when
  a text snapshot is genuinely ambiguous or the loop is retrying after a
  stuck detection.

## Locator resolution

`src/surface/locator-resolver.ts` implements the ladder + `verify` logic
described in schema-spec.mdc. It must:
- Try `primary`, then each `fallbacks[i]` in order.
- After resolving a candidate handle, run `verify` (role/text re-check).
  A candidate that fails `verify` counts as unresolved, not as a match.
- Report which tier ultimately succeeded (for drift telemetry), even on
  success via `primary`.
- Handle `Locator.frame` (iframe/frameset path) for legacy nested-frame
  layouts.

## Implementations

- `web-surface.ts`: Playwright + CDP. Uses
  `Accessibility.getFullAXTree` for perception,
  `Page.startScreencast` for the human-handoff live view (see the escalation
  spec), and `Input.dispatch*` to replay operator input onto the same page.
- `desktop-surface.stub.ts`: a stub only — do not implement full desktop
  automation for this assessment. It exists to prove the `Surface` interface
  doesn't paint the design into a corner: its methods should type-check and
  have a plausible (even if `throw new Error("not implemented")`) body, with
  a comment pointing to Windows UIA / macOS AX as the real backing
  technology.

## What NOT to do here

- Do not fall back to raw CSS selectors as the primary strategy just because
  it's easier to implement — that defeats the entire cross-surface
  portability argument this design rests on.
- Do not couple `Surface` to Playwright types outside `web-surface.ts`. The
  agent loop, recorder, and replay executor must only depend on the
  `Surface` interface.
